      "description": "It's the beginning of KimiK2 Valley's own turn 13 (not the opponent's upkeep), and the opponent has Tiger-Seal which taps at the beginning of their upkeep. KimiK2 Valley could have cast Burst Lightning targeting the Duelist of the Mind (a */3 whose power equals cards drawn this turn - currently 0/3 at start of turn, so 2 damage wouldn't kill it) or Tiger-Seal (3/3, also wouldn't die to 2 damage). However, casting Opt here during their own beginning step before their main phase is a missed opportunity - Opt at instant speed during their own upkeep/beginning step would let them see an extra card before deciding their main phase plays, and there's no reason to hold it since they have mana available. The reasoning mentions 'opponent's upkeep' but it's actually KimiK2 Valley's own turn beginning phase. While passing is not terrible, casting Opt here to dig for answers at 11 life facing two creatures is slightly better than waiting.",
      "action_taken": "Passed priority during their own beginning of turn step without casting any spells.",
      "better_line": "Cast Opt during their own beginning step to scry and draw before the main phase, improving information for land drop and spell sequencing decisions.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 125,
      "player": "KimiK2 Valley",
      "decision_index": 118,
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "Used Fear of Missing Out's Delirium untap trigger on the opponent's tapped Eddymurk Crab (5/5), giving the opponent a blocker they didn't have, instead of untapping one of their own tapped/attacking creatures to deal additional damage in the extra combat phase. While it triggers Duelist of the Mind's crime ability for a loot, that minor upside is vastly outweighed by giving the opponent a 5/5 blocker and losing extra combat damage.",
      "action_taken": "Targeted opponent's tapped Eddymurk Crab with the untap ability, giving the opponent a 5/5 blocker",
      "better_line": "Untap Tiger-Seal (3/3 vigilance, currently tapped) to attack with it in the additional combat phase for 3 extra damage, bringing opponent to 8 or lower. The crime trigger for Duelist's loot could be achieved through Into the Flood Maw in hand instead.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 127,
      "player": "Kimi25 Truth",
      "decision_index": 124,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Discarded Quantum Riddler, a powerful 4/6 flying finisher with card draw that wasn't yet on the battlefield, when they had redundant copies of enchantments already in play (second Stormchaser's Talent or second Frostcliff Siege) or a land (Spirebluff Canal) that could have been discarded instead.",
      "action_taken": "Discarded Quantum Riddler, mistakenly believing it was a duplicate of something on board.",
      "better_line": "Discard Spirebluff Canal (already at 6 lands) or the second Frostcliff Siege (redundant with one already on board), keeping Quantum Riddler as a strong threat to deploy later.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 128,
      "player": "Kimi25 Truth",
      "decision_index": 125,
    },
    {
      "severity": "questionable",
      "description": "Casting Into the Flood Maw targeting the tapped Eddymurk Crab during combat would commit a crime, triggering Duelist of the Mind's draw/discard ability to increase its power by 1 for the current combat. Additionally, bouncing the Crab forces the opponent to re-spend mana next turn. The extra point of combat damage matters with the opponent at 11 life.",
      "action_taken": "Declined to cast any instant during combat",
      "better_line": "Cast Into the Flood Maw (without gift) targeting Eddymurk Crab to bounce it, trigger Duelist's crime ability for +1 power during combat, and gain tempo advantage",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 129,
      "player": "Kimi25 Truth",
      "decision_index": 125,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Into the Flood Maw targeting Eddymurk Crab would commit a crime, triggering Duelist of the Mind's draw/discard ability. Drawing a card would set Duelist's power to 1, allowing it to deal combat damage, which would then trigger Frostcliff Siege (Jeskai) to draw another card. This chain generates meaningful card advantage and also removes the opponent's 5/5 creature temporarily.",
      "action_taken": "Declined to cast either instant during combat, likely resulting in Duelist of the Mind dealing 0 combat damage and missing the Frostcliff Siege draw trigger.",
      "better_line": "Cast Into the Flood Maw targeting Eddymurk Crab (with or without gift) to commit a crime, trigger Duelist's draw/discard, pump Duelist to at least 1 power for combat damage, and trigger Frostcliff Siege's card draw.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 129,
      "player": "Kimi25 Truth",
      "decision_index": 125,
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "KimiK2 Valley is facing lethal across two combats due to Fear of Missing Out's delirium granting an extra combat phase. Total damage across both combats would be ~17, far exceeding their 11 life. They had 3 untapped lands and 2 Burst Lightnings in hand, which together deal 4 damage - enough to kill Fear of Missing Out (2/3) before it attacks, preventing the extra combat phase. Without the extra combat, only Duelist (2) + Quantum Riddler (4) = 6 damage, leaving them at 5 life.",
      "action_taken": "Passed priority during combat, allowing all three creatures including Fear of Missing Out to attack unimpeded, resulting in lethal damage across two combat phases.",
      "better_line": "Cast both Burst Lightnings targeting Fear of Missing Out (dealing 4 total damage to a 3-toughness creature) to kill it before attacks, preventing the delirium extra combat trigger and reducing incoming damage to 6 instead of lethal.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 129,
      "player": "KimiK2 Valley",
      "decision_index": 127,
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "KimiK2 Valley is at 3 life facing lethal combat damage from Tiger-Seal (3/3) and Duelist of the Mind (3/3) attacking unblocked. They have Eddymurk Crab (5/5) on the battlefield which could block one attacker, plus Burst Lightning in hand which could kill the other 3-toughness creature. They also have an Opt they could cast before blocks. Instead of blocking or using removal, they passed on casting instants during combat and took 3 damage from Tiger-Seal alone, going to 0 life. They had Burst Lightning available to kill Tiger-Seal or Duelist of the Mind before damage, and Eddymurk Crab to block the other. This was a survivable combat that they chose not to interact with.",
      "action_taken": "Passed on casting any instants during combat, taking lethal damage.",
      "better_line": "Cast Burst Lightning targeting Tiger-Seal (dealing 2 damage to a 3/3... wait, that doesn't kill it). Actually, Burst Lightning deals 2 damage which doesn't kill a 3/3. But Eddymurk Crab (5/5) should have been declared as a blocker on one of the attackers. The decision point shown is during combat before blockers are declared or during the declare blockers step - they should block one attacker with Eddymurk Crab to survive. Actually, looking more carefully, KimiK2 Valley is at 3 life and both attackers are unblocked dealing 6 total - they die. They needed to block with Eddymurk Crab and couldn't kill either 3/3 with Burst Lightning alone. But blocking one with Eddymurk Crab would reduce damage to 3, still lethal. They needed to cast both Burst Lightnings on one creature (4 damage total, killing it) and block the other with Eddymurk Crab. Or cast Opt to try to find an answer. At minimum, they should have tried something rather than accepting death.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 131,
      "player": "KimiK2 Valley",
      "decision_index": 128,
    }
  ],
